-- title:
--   Kernel of a surjective Hopf quotient is generated by the augmentation ideal of the Hopf kernel
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain (a commutative ring that is a domain and in which every ideal is principal), and let $A$ and $B$ be commutative rings carrying Hopf algebra structures over $R$, each finite and flat as an $R$-module, with the comultiplication of $A$ cocommutative. Let $\pi \colon A \to B$ be a morphism of $R$-bialgebras and assume the underlying function is surjective. Write $\mathrm{hopfKer}\,\pi$ for the $R$-subalgebra of $A$ on which the algebra map $(\mathrm{id}_A \otimes \pi) \circ \Delta_A \colon A \to A \otimes_R B$ agrees with $a \mapsto a \otimes 1$, and let $\ker \varepsilon_A$ be the kernel of the counit of $A$, viewed as an algebra map $A \to R$. The assertion is that the kernel of $\pi$ as a ring homomorphism equals the ideal of $A$ generated by the set $\mathrm{hopfKer}\,\pi \cap \ker \varepsilon_A$; in classical notation, $\ker \pi = C^{+}A$ where $C = A^{\mathrm{co}B}$ is the Hopf kernel and $C^{+} = C \cap \ker \varepsilon_A$ is its augmentation ideal.
--
--   In the language of affine group schemes over $R$, this identifies the closed subscheme cut out by the Hopf kernel $C = A^{\mathrm{co}B}$ with the kernel of the quotient map, one half of the Takeuchi correspondence between Hopf ideals and sub-Hopf algebras. It is used in the analysis of towers of $p$-divisible groups, where a transition map is shown to vanish exactly on the ideal generated by an image of the Hopf kernel.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_HopfAlgebra_ker_eq_map_hopfKer_inf_ker_counit_of_surjective.lean

